  "body": "Stacked on #1139 (on #1123, #560, #551) - this diff shows their commits until they merge; merge #551, #560, #1123, #1139 first. Fork synced at v0.18.0. Not exercised on a real EDM control.\n\n**Integration with #1139 (#533 wire post).** `issue/533-wire-iso-post` is merged into this branch (`8cd0aff6`), keeping both sides: `posting_wire.rs` holds #533's Wire Contour stream and this PR's wire clause, mounted once in `posting.rs` as `wire` with the clause re-exported; `wire_contour.rs` asserts the combined truth (the note points at the wire laws, and the wire cut streams with its condition). With a real wire post underneath, the verdict now stands between a judged cut and NC text: `wire_post::the_wire_laws_stand_between_the_cut_and_its_nc_text` (`3e852d68`) posts the clean punch through `wire-iso.cps` to #533's golden (unchanged, byte-identical) and refuses a die whose kerf burns the plate by name (`Die1: wire gouge: the kerf of move ...`), writing no NC file.\n\n## What changed\n\nWire EDM verification of a 2-axis cut (#534, part of #290 D7). Every finding blocks the post; nothing is a warning.\n\n**Laws** - `crates/sim/src/verify/wire/` (pure 2D, `judge(&WireJob) -> WireVerdict`):\n- **Kerf law (gouge).** Each move's segment must stay at least the kerf offset (wire radius + spark gap, from the condition) from the product's section, and must not stand in it, at every judged height. The first move that breaks it is named with its depth, the spot, the height and how many moves gouge. The check uses exact point-to-segment distance, not an area threshold.\n- **Threading law.** The start hole must be stated, the path must start at it, and the product must stand clear of it by the kerf offset at every height through the full stock height. Each failure is refused by name.\n- **Slug law.** The kerf (the offset disc swept along the whole path, `Region::sweep`) is taken out of the material section (stock and part), and the connected pieces inside the cut's filled outline are the ones freed. A die whose freed piece holds part material (\"the part drops\") is refused, and so is a punch whose freed pieces hold none (\"only waste drops\"). A cut that frees nothing is refused too.\n- **What \"the part\" is, by the stated side.** For a punch it is the model. For a die it is the model when the start hole stands outside it (a die plate with the opening as its hole). When the start hole stands inside the model, the model is the opening (#532's whole-model die) and the stock around it is the product.\n\n**Gate** - `crates/app`:\n- `operation/wire_verify.rs` gathers the inputs live, at the post. That is the cached path, the condition's offset, the side and start, and the part and stock sliced at both ends of the span the wire passes plus about every 2 mm between (at most 9 heights). The span covers the op's planes, the part and the stock, whichever reaches farthest.\n- `posting_wire.rs` adds the wire clause to `verification_report`, beside the plasma clause. Every finding is a blocking problem. The not-judged line says the 3D stock sim did not run for the wire, and names the piece each cut frees: \"Die1 frees 7xx mm² near X0 Y0 (waste)\" or \"Punch1 frees ... (the part)\".\n- A wire cut taken from the stock, or one with no path, is refused by name at the gate.\n- The machine check's wire note no longer says verification is not built. It now points to the post's wire laws, and the one assertion in `wire_contour.rs` that read the old note was updated to match.\n\nDecisions in `docs/decisions/534-wire-verify.md`. They cover: judged live at the post, the whole height the wire passes, exact distance rather than area, the product reading per side, threading clearance = the kerf offset, slug connectivity, and no capture/tab law until #539.\n\n## How tested\n\n- `camcraft-sim` lib, `verify::wire::tests` (5): `a_correct_punch_passes_and_drops_the_punch`, `a_correct_die_passes_whether_the_model_is_the_opening_or_the_plate`, `a_kerf_into_the_part_is_a_gouge_naming_the_move`, `a_missing_moved_or_crowded_start_hole_is_refused`, `the_slug_is_read_for_the_stated_side` (a punch stated on a die plate's hole, a die stated on a punch outline, and a die plate with no stock).\n- `camcraft-app` `wire_verify` (3), through the commands and the gate: `a_correct_die_and_a_correct_punch_pass_and_name_the_piece_they_free`, `a_kerf_into_the_part_blocks_the_post_naming_the_move` (a 12 mm opening model in a 20 mm plate, so above the opening the wire burns the plate), `a_missing_start_hole_blocks_the_post`.\n- `wire_contour` (7) still green, with the machine note's assertion updated.\n- `cargo xtask gate quick --base upstream/issue/532-wire-contour` on the final head `82bf9b54`: **green**, 4 steps and 0 failed (sim 19 s, motion 9 s, app 602 s for the whole suite list including `wire_contour` and `wire_verify`, ui 90 s).\n- `cargo clippy -p camcraft-sim -p camcraft-app --all-targets -- -D warnings`: clean.\n- After merging #1139: `cargo xtask gate quick --base origin/issue/533-wire-iso-post` on head `3e852d68`: **green**, 4 steps, 0 failed (sim 12 s, motion 4 s, app 522 s incl. `wire_post` 5, `wire_contour` 7, `wire_verify` 3, ui 86 s); clippy -D warnings on sim/app clean.\n\n## Not exercised\n\n- **Not exercised on a real EDM control.** The condition is synthetic, and nothing was cut.\n- On this PR alone (without #1139) there is no wire post; the integration above is what puts the verdict in front of NC text.\n- Taper (#536): the laws read straight, vertical, unposed moves and refuse anything else by name. The ruled surface between planes is not judged.\n- Capture/tabs (#539, D6): a correct slug passes and is named on the report line. An unsupported slug is not refused, because no capture parameter exists yet.\n- A pre-drilled start hole's diameter is not modelled (the op states a point). \"Too small\" means the product stands within the kerf offset of the threading point.\n- Only the op's source model is judged. Another model on the same setup that stands in the wire's column is not checked.\n- A selection-sourced (picked-edge) wire cut was not tested through the gate.\n\nChangelog: Wire EDM: a Wire Contour is verified at the post in 2.5D: a kerf into the part (naming the move), a missing or crowded start hole, and a slug on the wrong side (die vs punch) or a cut that frees nothing each block the post by name, and the report names the piece each cut frees (#534)\n\nEffort: high (not escalated)\n\nFixes #534\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n",
